<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Padmore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malcolm Ivan Meredith Nurse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Padmore was a Trinidadian Pan-Africanist, journalist and author. A Communist Party member from 1927, he headed the Negro Bureau of the Red International of Labour Unions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and edited The Negro Worker, but broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> in 1933 over what he saw as its flagging support for colonial independence. Settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1934, he chaired the International African Service Bureau and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Fifth Pan-African Congress</a> at Manchester in 1945. He advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, where his ashes were buried after his death in London.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-padmore.jpg" alt="George Padmore" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Padmore,_circa_1937.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Malcolm Ivan Meredith Nurse was born on 28 June 1903 in Arouca District, Tacarigua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. His father, James Hubert Alfonso Nurse, was a local schoolmaster, and his paternal great-grandfather was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> warrior sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>. After school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-of-spain">Port of Spain</a> he worked for several years as a reporter with the Trinidad Publishing Company. In late 1924 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to study medicine at Fisk University, later moving to New York University and then Howard University. He took the name George Padmore for party business, joining the Communist Party in 1927 and working in its American Negro Labor Congress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1929 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> asked Padmore to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to head the Negro Bureau of the Red International of Labour Unions, and he was elected to the Moscow City Soviet. In July 1930 he helped organise a conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> that launched the International Trade Union Committee of Negro Workers, and he edited its monthly, The Negro Worker, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and then in Hamburg. After the Nazi seizure of power the paper's offices were ransacked and he was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Disillusioned by the Comintern's pursuit of alliances with colonial powers, he cut his ties in 1933 and was expelled from the movement on 23 February 1934.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1934 he became the centre of a community of Pan-Africanist writers. He chaired the International African Service Bureau, which grew out of C. L. R. James's group on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amy-ashwood-garvey">Amy Ashwood Garvey</a>. His books included How Britain Rules Africa (1936) and Africa and World Peace (1937). In 1945 he organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-pan-african-congress">Manchester Pan-African Congress</a>, attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, which helped set the agenda for post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Padmore advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a> by letter, wrote for his newspaper and published The Gold Coast Revolution (1953) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a>? (1956). He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> at Nkrumah's invitation, but his time there was difficult, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for treatment of cirrhosis of the liver. He died on 23 September 1959 at University College Hospital. His ashes were buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osu-castle">Christiansborg Castle</a> on 4 October 1959.</p><p>Nkrumah opened the George Padmore Research Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> on 30 June 1961. The George Padmore Institute, an archive and research centre in North London, was founded in 1991 by John La Rose, and a blue plaque marks his former home at 22 Cranleigh Street in Camden.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Padmore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In London C. L. R. James, Amy Ashwood Garvey and others formed the International African Friends of Abyssinia, and in 1937 <b>George Padmore</b> led its successor, the International African Service Bureau.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arouca, in what is now <a href="/countries/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a>. Died in University College Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.63,-61.33&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Arouca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2860 1767 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Padmore"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3310 2037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3310 2037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.63,-61.33&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
